WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.827
What is vRAN or Virtual Radio Access Network?

00:00:03.827 --> 00:00:05.821
How does it compare to traditional RAN?

00:00:05.821 --> 00:00:08.274
Hi, I'm Dan Kehn from IBM Cloud,

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:11.698
and before I answer&nbsp;those questions, please click like and subscribe.

00:00:11.698 --> 00:00:17.405
The short answer is vRAN is network functionality&nbsp;
delivered as software instead of hardware.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.057
Communication Service Providers (CSPs) are already&nbsp;
investing in 5G,

00:00:22.057 --> 00:00:27.200
so the timing is good to be able to think about other ways they&nbsp;
can make their networks more flexible.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.427
That's where Virtual Radio Access Networks,&nbsp;
which follow the Open RAN architecture, can help.

00:00:32.627 --> 00:00:36.502
To better understand vRAN it helps to compare&nbsp;
them side by side,

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:41.196
vRAN versus traditional RAN.

00:00:41.639 --> 00:00:47.006
So, we've all seen a traditional site of a cell&nbsp;
tower up there as an antenna.

00:00:47.006 --> 00:00:52.774
Along with the antenna is a Remote Radio Unit, or RU for short.

00:00:52.774 --> 00:00:58.542
Down on the ground there's a different device called a Base Band Unit, or BBU.

00:00:58.542 --> 00:01:01.755
The BBU takes&nbsp;the signals from the RU

00:01:01.755 --> 00:01:05.987
and then forwards it on to the next place in the chain, the central&nbsp;
unit.

00:01:05.987 --> 00:01:10.198
The central unit is connected to multiple BBUs within a small region

00:01:10.198 --> 00:01:13.972
and concentrates&nbsp;them to forward it on to the core network.

00:01:14.563 --> 00:01:18.424
So, for vRAN, the picture starts out basically&nbsp;
the same:

00:01:18.424 --> 00:01:21.543
we have a tower antenna and RU

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.650
but what's different is, instead of&nbsp;the BBU,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:30.295
we have a different device called a VDU, or Virtual Distribution Unit. 

00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:37.954
It&nbsp;then connects to a different piece of equipment&nbsp;called a VCU, or Virtual Central Unit,

00:01:37.954 --> 00:01:42.195
which&nbsp;connects then to the core network just as before.

00:01:42.472 --> 00:01:47.326
So, on the surface, these look pretty much&nbsp;
the same but there are some key differences.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.045
The BBU is a fixed piece of hardware, proprietary&nbsp;
with fixed capacity.

00:01:53.206 --> 00:01:58.881
On the other hand, the VDU and VCU, it runs on commercial off-the-shelf hardware.

00:01:58.881 --> 00:02:04.775
And then, installed on that, is software called a VNF, or Virtual Network Function.

00:02:04.775 --> 00:02:12.366
That&nbsp;provides the same functionality as the BBU but using software on commercial off-the-shelf&nbsp;
hardware.

00:02:12.366 --> 00:02:17.960
So, now with that quick overview, let's look at 3 different scenarios to see how&nbsp;they differ.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.840
The first one I call "Road Trip".

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.120
So, let's imagine that you're driving down&nbsp;
the highway, you're listening to music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.846
on your favorite streaming service.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.977
That&nbsp;signal is coming from this cell tower

00:02:35.409 --> 00:02:39.676
and, unfortunately, you run across a traffic jam.

00:02:39.676 --> 00:02:44.529
Now,&nbsp;you and the other driver is going to want to think about ways you perhaps avoid this traffic jam.

00:02:44.529 --> 00:02:46.503
So,&nbsp;you bring up your navigation app,

00:02:46.503 --> 00:02:49.716
you might even call your family and say you're going to be late,

00:02:49.716 --> 00:02:56.058
resulting in lots of different signals hitting the same tower, potentially overwhelming this BBU.

00:02:56.058 --> 00:02:59.882
The scenario plays out a little bit differently in the case of vRAN.

00:02:59.882 --> 00:03:03.273
Now, I showed just one VDU in&nbsp;the initial drawing,

00:03:03.273 --> 00:03:11.020
but more realistically a CSP would install multiple VDUs in what's called&nbsp;a "VDU cluster".

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.008
So, that means they can handle the situation much more elegantly

00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:19.113
because they can&nbsp;bring on additional capacity on demand.

00:03:19.113 --> 00:03:26.829
So, let's look now at another scenario, which is not quite&nbsp;
so unpredictable this road trip, I call "Downtown".

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.449
So, we have a cluster of buildings

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.867
and with that cluster of&nbsp;buildings we have lots of people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.116 --> 00:03:39.695
Because there's lots of people,

00:03:39.695 --> 00:03:42.835
we can make&nbsp;predictions about what their usage patterns&nbsp;might be,

00:03:42.835 --> 00:03:46.909
and so that means we'll be&nbsp;able to plan, essentially, or automate

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:49.133
what capacity we need at what times.

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:52.058
For example,&nbsp; if we have office workers that come in the morning,

00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:58.233
take lunch, and then leave in the evening, we might&nbsp;
see rises in demand at those specific times.

00:03:58.233 --> 00:04:01.770
But in the evening we may only need minimal capacity

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:07.323
and thus we can apply the level of capacity that's required given the demand.

00:04:07.323 --> 00:04:11.216
OK, there's&nbsp;one final scenario that i would like to cover.

00:04:11.216 --> 00:04:15.489
One that doesn't involve monitoring the dynamic&nbsp;
nature of the network.

00:04:15.675 --> 00:04:18.000
I call this one "Campus".

00:04:19.241 --> 00:04:23.280
So, we've left the highway, we're&nbsp;
no longer downtown, we're on campus.

00:04:25.352 --> 00:04:31.920
And on this campus they have&nbsp;
a cell tower.  It's serving 5G.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.939
The dean of this campus wants to provide wi-fi&nbsp;
capability throughout the campus.

00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:44.393
Not just in the buildings, but anywhere outside, so they can have&nbsp;
outdoor classrooms, for example.

00:04:44.393 --> 00:04:47.257
So, the CSP can make an offer to them.

00:04:47.257 --> 00:04:52.963
They suggest that they install&nbsp;a wi-fi capable antenna on the existing tower

00:04:52.963 --> 00:04:58.039
and because the VDU is software based, that&nbsp;
means they can install new services.

00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:03.588
In this case, a wi-fi capable VNF.

00:05:03.588 --> 00:05:09.562
That wi-fi capability can&nbsp;be brought up during the day while students are there and be used actively.

00:05:09.562 --> 00:05:14.647
And in the evening, where the demand drops off, they can also reduce the capacity that it provides

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:21.813
therefore offering a&nbsp;lot more flexibility in how they deliver services.

00:05:22.213 --> 00:05:29.440
And here we offered a lot more dynamic&nbsp;
way of addressing our capacity demands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.858
Together, these two really paint a picture&nbsp;
of automation.

00:05:35.858 --> 00:05:38.735
Now, automation, or network automation,

00:05:38.735 --> 00:05:42.096
is really a separate topic in and of&nbsp;itself and deserves its own video,

00:05:42.096 --> 00:05:45.368
so please look below in the description for links about it.

00:05:45.368 --> 00:05:46.869
Thank&nbsp;you for watching.

00:05:46.869 --> 00:05:49.762
If you have questions, please drop us a line below.

00:05:49.762 --> 00:05:54.697
If you want to see more videos&nbsp;
like this in the future, please like and subscribe.

